findings.body:
**Critical: password-reset tokens are no longer cryptographically secure.**

`Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12)` yields at most ~10^12 values (~40 bits) from a non-CSPRNG. The previous `randomBytes(32)` produced 256 bits of secure entropy. An attacker can brute-force or predict reset tokens and take over accounts.

For Edge compatibility, use Web Crypto instead of `Math.random`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

`crypto.getRandomValues` is available in Edge runtimes, browsers, and modern Node.
